    "question": {
      ":question/id": "25582b9a-89f5-5a4c-bfd3-907cdb4e1695",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = 2 - xy$?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(-1,2)$,$(1,3)$,$(0,-2)$.\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,2)}=2-1(-1)(2)=4, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,3)}=2-1(1)(3)=-1, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,-2)}=2-1(0)(-2)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](7d7320693894e1727875d22ac02f8463dca16e3ae3fd1875cfdf39dafc665d4e.png)",
