    "question": {
      ":question/id": "5a4d7b2b-4f6b-5510-b0ab-b61f7b4995b1",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = x^{2} - 2y$?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(1,2)$,$(-1,-1)$,$(2,-2)$.\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,2)}=1(1)^{2}-2(2)=-3, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,-1)}=1(-1)^{2}-2(-1)=3, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,-2)}=1(2)^{2}-2(-2)=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](aee3e787d69c7585820db56bf672a7b8e87d6369824535c41bebce2c6d2f8450.png)",
